'' String lowering against the ABI measured in docs/internals/phase-6-rtl.md: a
'' var-length string is passed as a POINTER TO ITS DESCRIPTOR with a length of -1.
''
'' A '+' chain flattens into ONE assign followed by concat-assigns, which is what fbc
'' emits -- 'a = b + c' needs no temporary at all. One is needed only when the
'' DESTINATION appears on the right ('a = b + a'), because building in place would
'' overwrite the source before it is read; that shape is skipped rather than lowered
'' into a store over 24-byte descriptors, which would be wrong rather than partial.
''
'' fb_StrDelete goes through the cleanup list, so it inherits the order measured from
'' fbc: reverse declaration, and every open scope on an early RETURN.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/strings.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: local agg a size 24
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_StrAssign(@a, -1, @b, -1, 0)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_StrConcatAssign(@a, -1, @b, -1, 0)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_StrDelete(@a)
'' EXPECT-OUT: global str $str0 "x"
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_StrAssign(@a, -1, @@$str0, 2, 0)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_StrConcat(@$tmp2, @b, -1, @a, -1)
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' GOLDEN: expected/strings.out

'' fb_StrCompare, then the operator applied against ZERO -- measured from fbc:
''     int32 vr$5 = fb_StrCompare( &A$1, -1ll, &B$1, -1ll );
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl i32 fb_StrCompare(@a, -1, @b, -1)
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.eq %10, 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.slt
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.sge

'' The literal length INCLUDES the terminator here -- 4 for "lit" -- because this call
'' follows the fb_StrAssign convention, not the exact-length one fb_StrAllocTempDescZEx
'' takes. The two conventions sit one function apart.
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_StrCompare(@a, -1, @@$str1, 4)
